this is a production of cornell  university  uh so today i'm going to be talking  about the malice venturi patha system  and how host adaptation and the  geographical isolation  leads to population differentiation and  the impact that it has on  post-resistance breeding in apples  so  the fungus venturi necklace is aspirin  asparagus  fungus  that's the causal organism of apple scab  it causes olive to green or olive green  to black lesions on the leaves  and these black cracked creams on apples  this is a very significant disease in  apples  producers have to undergo really  extensive management practices in order  to control it  because these cracks severely reduce the  fruit quality and can just completely  decimate produce yield  the spores have a very limited dispersal  area  but they do undergo a sexual phase in  their yearly life cycle  and frequent recombinations lead to high  variability within the fungus  so for management of apple scab  like i said it's a really significant  disease and primarily management focuses  on  proper organ sanitation so making sure  that  leaf litter is removed at the end of the  season uh because that is where the  sexual recombination occurs for the  fungus so proper removal of leaves and  destruction of that uh  inoculum is really important  careful fungicide application to make  sure you're not leading to fungicide  resistant populations and then also  developing resistant cultivars it's  really important  for stable long-term management  especially for organic growers who don't  necessarily have the same chemical tools  that conventional growers do  so  in apples  there are currently 18 identified  major genes  that are resistant to apple scab  we call these rbi  and  there's a gene for gene interaction  between these  malice rvi genes with  pathogen avr genes  these effective proteins trigger the  downstream immune response  and about 90 of scab-resistant cultivars  rely on a single gene rbi6  this is originally identified in malice  floribunda 821  this resistance has since been broken  uh it's been repeatedly identified in  europe uh but last year it was actually  identified here in new york as the first  case in north america  this is really a cost for concern  that rbi gene  is in a tight gene cluster on focus  group one  and another one of my projects is  looking at another  candidate host resistance gene from  honeycrisp that's also located in this  type gene cluster  between rbi 6 and rbi 17  so apple domestication originally  started in the tunisian mountains and  what's now kazakhstan uh  and  that's the center of origin for the  primary progenitor species for apples  malice versailles  malu subversii was brought along the  silk road trading route where it  hybridized with other apple species in  the area  primarily malice orientalis in the  caucasus area and malucildestrus in  europe  this hybridization was really important  for the  co-evolution of venturi necklace because  the  post-geographical movement  towards europe as well as the  hybridization with other local species  led to three really identifiable  populations of the eye  so there's the central asian mountain  population that's only found in the  chanshan origin of malus aversion  there's the central asian plains  population which can infect malice of  rci and malice domestica which are our  moderate domesticated apples  and then there's also an eu population  that only is identifiable  in europe and  uh these populations can overcome mouse  silvestris and mouse or mouse domestica  so these three populations are really  clearly identified well reported on  um but one of the things that we are  really interested in is looking to see  if the post differentiation and  geographic isolation  of north american isolates led to a new  unique population  so  we're looking at the geographical  isolation between europe and the united  states  as well as any host adaptation that  might have occurred with north american  isolates  so we started out with 164  isolates that we collected both  from ncbi they're probably available and  from our lab  we have  illumina short read sequences from them  we removed 41 of the isolates  either due to poor sample quality or  contamination determined by the mating  type locus analysis  and so we aligned these sequences  against the ncbi reference genome  we filtered out the alignment reads for  quality  and then we conducted snip calling with  the  gap k haplotype collar  so we ended up with a little over 19  million raw snips and we underwent snip  filtration over a couple of stages to  make sure that we weren't losing out  quality sniffs uh as we were going  through  these full productions  so we started off with just some very  basic  filter parameters to try and remove the  lowest quality sites  i brought us down to about 4.4 million  uh and then we wanted to look at  population level snips um to bring that  data set down to a workable  range so  that brought us down to 375 000  and then finally we did hard  heterozygosity filtering because venturi  is a haploid fungus  and so any heterozygous sites uh would  just be due to sequencing error  so principal component analysis appears  to show uh  our north american isolates starting to  form their own distinct subpopulation  you can clearly see other populations  identified that are due to both host uh  geographic adaptation and  uh  sorry geographic isolation and host  adaptation  uh you can see the european malice and  it's covered up by the zoom bar up there  and then the two other identified  populations the uh cam and cat  populations that's the  central asian plains central asian  mountains  as well as the population  that was obtained on malus orientalists  so pci  appears to show uh a population  developing  however  further bayesian analysis shows that  there's not enough statistical support  to show that these population or these  isolates have formed their own  true population yet uh what it likely  means is that there just hasn't been  enough time for that population to  develop um  apple domestication took a took place  over a range of thousands of years  but  these isolates only been in north  america for the last few hundred  so we really just haven't seen  these isolates form their own distinct  population yet  and also  it was the process of  both the host adaptation due to  hybridization and the geographical  movement that led to these distinct  categories so i think that also played a  role in why we haven't seen a true  population arise yet  but looking at just isolates that we  collected in geneva new york  up around agritech  we actually did identify three  subpopulations within a single orchard  and the key characteristic that defines  these subpopulations is the ability to  overcome that key resistance gene rbi6  so two of the populations have the  ability to overcome  um  but there are two very distinct  populations that have that  characteristic  so what that likely means is that either  two resistance overcoming events  happened in the same orchard  or an isolate that can overcome was  introduced later on  and this orchard did not have any kind  of  disease management  so that probably also  is very significant for allowing that  selection pressure to come out  so just kind of wrapping up  these north american ices haven't yet  formed a distinct population just due to  the limited time that they've had in  isolation from other  key populations  um  and this work in the future can help  identify those pathogen avr genes  which are really important for  identifying new populations of the  pathogen that can overcome  host resistance as breeders continue to  develop new resistant cultivars  and also identifying the snips that  occur in these effectors can also help  us try and understand how  uh the host uh adaptation of the  geographic isolation  actually led to these population  differentiation and how this might  happen again in the future  i'd like to thank the  con lab for their support um as well as  the other two members of my committee  sarah pat the bridge and lance kadel  davidson  i have a question yes um how long do you  think will isolation of new culture  verse take in like a time frame like how  long do you think it would take for a  u.s or north american population to form  or um like even to spread through the us  with like a time scale  not a hundred percent sure and part of  the reason why  that might not be the case is uh just  due to how  really isolates are able to move between  areas now just due to  global trade  so originally that limited sport  dispersal played a key role in why  the isils were able to slowly evolve  with the co-domestication process  but now isolates can travel between  europe and north america in a few hours  along with defective materials  so  rapidly being able to go back and forth  might mean that they might not ever uh  form their own unique population  can you  transfer the disease with if you bring  like an alpha apple fruit  is it an infected apple fruit from  europe to the us for example or does it  have to be meat material  um no you can bring  infected fruits as well but typically  affected fruit isn't transferred as  often  the blemishes that applescrap causes are  so devastating to the fruit price uh  then most farmers don't even bother  trying to harvest them they identify  with the field  thank you  yes  so do you have an enough understanding  of the  effectors and the translations to be  able to start structurally modeling it  you know at the level of protein-protein  interactions  uh not yet um  part of that is that uh venturi  effectors are still uh  a very  emerging uh area of study uh we just  received  uh  a set of candidate effector sequences  from uh carl mezarek in new zealand and  we have annotation data for uh these  snips in this reference genome so part  of what we're going to try and do next  is look at the  uh  the snips within these candidate  effector regions and look at the  predicted effects uh they have on the  protein  uh translation  uh and hopefully then we might have a  little bit better but not quite there  yet  so you do you think they affect the  range translation or the actual protein  structure  the protein structure  exactly it was kind of is you know where  we are now with you know proteins  predict their structure  yeah this whole question be able to dock  things you don't have to crystallize now  thank you  and that's applause for everyone who  spoke today  this has been a production of cornell  university on the web at cornell.edu

